                                                            <rect key="frame" x="0.0" y="24.333333333333371" width="264" height="446.33333333333331"/>
                                                            <string key="text">In iOS development, if you want to apply a color only to the top portion of a view instead of coloring the entire background, you can achieve this by adding a separate subview with the desired color and positioning it at the top using Auto Layout constraints. This technique allows you to create a header-like effect or highlight the upper section of the screen without affecting the rest of the view. By setting the background color of the subview and adjusting its height (e.g., 60 points), you can precisely control how much of the top area is colored. This method is flexible and works well whether you're designing your interface programmatically or using Interface Builder in Storyboard.</string>
                                                            <fontDescription key="fontDescription" type="system" pointSize="17"/>
                                                            <nil key="textColor"/>
                                                            <nil key="highlightedColor"/>
                                                        </label>
